{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790535502,"nonce":"FqSWXN/4sppedxyqdu1I3VrvugA=","target":"7f446e887894628efd67b8635129b6239ee2f2350fa106c559a1df26eaf46300","message":"The isolate module is registered only with the host feature, because native isolates start a std::thread and wasm32-wasip1 has none. So a wasm AOT program has no Isolate or Channel. Nothing else stands in the way: the wasm runtime object carries the parser, the MIR pipeline and the interpreter (VM::interpret), so a wasm AOT program can load a module from source and run it as a native VM does. d51592b covers the browser build (the embedded runtime); this issue is the AOT program's side of the same model, with the behaviour isolates have natively.\n\nDesign:\n1. An isolate is an agent: a Worker in the browser, a thread under wasmtime or a native host. Isolate.spawn asks the host, through a host import, to instantiate the same .wasm on a new agent. A fresh instance has fresh linear memory, so its heap is its own. Isolates share nothing, so this needs no wasm threads and no shared heap.\n2. Entry. Export wlift_isolate_run(vm, module, arg). It runs the named module with arg as IsolateCore.arg:\n   - A module the build compiled installs and runs as compiled code (the bootstrap already goes module by module).\n   - Any other module is loaded as source through a loader host import and interpreted by the same runtime. Under wasmtime that reads WASI files; in the browser it fetches, or reads sources the build bundled.\n3. Compile-time resolution is the fast path, not a restriction. The walker treats Isolate.spawn(\u003cstring literal\u003e, ...) as another root, when Isolate is the isolate module's class, and compiles the named module and its imports. A spawned module is its own root, so it does not need to come before the module that spawns it. A module name computed at runtime still works, interpreted.\n4. Channels. Xfer gets a byte encoding. send, receive and join go through host imports, the way ash_host_dlopen/dlsym are forwarded, so each host brings its own transport:\n   - With SharedArrayBuffer (a cross-origin isolated page, or wasmtime): channel queues live in shared memory separate from every instance's, and receive/join block with Atomics.wait.\n   - The browser's main thread may not wait, so the program's main isolate runs in a Worker too, as wlift.js's worker mode already does.\n   - Without SharedArrayBuffer: postMessage, with receive/join parking the fiber and returning to the event loop (d51592b items 2-3).\n\nSet aside: running isolates on wlift.js's embedded runtime, with a buffer shared between it and the AOT program.\n- The two builds each have their own allocator and collector, so a shared buffer could only safely carry channel queues, not a heap.\n- The program would ship a second runtime.\n- wlift.js exists only in the browser, while wasm AOT also targets wasmtime and Ash's page host.\nThe interpreter the AOT runtime already carries covers the modules the build did not compile.","files":null}]}